# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2 has "free_will → ◇¬foreseen_happens" and "impious_to_deny → god_foreknows"; boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2.2 and boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2.3 have "free_will → ◇¬foreseen_happens"; they differ when only "there is freedom of the will", "to think that God has only uncertain conjecture of the future is impiety" and "possibly, it is not so that that which providence foresees to be about to happen comes to pass" are true, which the second allows
boethius-foreknowledge.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2 has "¬free_will"; boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2.2 and boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2.3 have "¬(god_foreknows ∧ free_will)"; they differ when only "there is freedom of the will" is true, which the second allows
boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2 puts it at S3, "Wherefore, if from eternity He foreknows not only what men will do, but also their designs and purposes, there can be no freedom of the will, seeing that nothing can be done, nor can any sort of purpose be entertained, save such as a Divine providence, incapable of being deceived, has perceived beforehand."; boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2.2 and boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2.3 at S1, "'It seems,' said I, 'too much of a paradox and a contradiction that God should know all things, and yet there should be free will."
boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2: argue/answered: M: answered no, which keeps boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2.2 and boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2.3, to: Is the text's main conclusion S3, "Wherefore, if from eternity He foreknows not only what men will do, but also their designs and purposes, there can be no freedom of the will, seeing that nothing can be done, nor can any sort of purpose be entertained, save such as a Divine providence, incapable of being deceived, has perceived beforehand.", rather than S1, "'It seems,' said I, 'too much of a paradox and a contradiction that God should know all things, and yet there should be free will."?
boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2 takes for granted "if to think that God has only uncertain conjecture of the future is impiety, then God foresees everything from eternity, men's deeds and their designs and purposes alike, and can in no wise be deceived"; boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2.2 and boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2.3 do not
boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps boethius-foreknowledge.logic.r2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if to think that God has only uncertain conjecture of the future is impiety, then God foresees everything from eternity, men's deeds and their designs and purposes alike, and can in no wise be deceived"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
